Four stale claims on the reference shelf, all of them contradicted by files
in the same folder.

- for_legislators.md § 1: 'One of the six names officers' and 'Connecticut
  ... is the first American statute to write corporate roles into a frontier
  provision'. E20 established on 21 August that California SB 53 (chaptered
  29 Sep 2025) does the same thing and the tally is two. Restated as two,
  both named, with the point sharpened: the recurrence is the finding, not
  the primacy -- two legislatures, fourteen months apart, independently
  decided officers should be told, and neither asked the officer told to do
  anything.
- frontier_bill_census.md, Connecticut row: E20 corrected the tally but not
  this row's VERDICT sentence, which is the quotable one and still claimed
  firstness 350 lines from its own disproof. Conformed.
- frontier_self_reporting_note.md § 1: 'Only the first is mandated anywhere:
  California ... and New York ...' -- naming two of the three framework
  statutes while citing, in the same sentence, the file in the same folder
  that pins all three. Illinois 430 ILCS 185/10 added.
- for_legislators.md § 2: the framework arithmetic ran three plus two plus
  six, accounting for eleven of twelve. Microsoft restored to the middle
  group (a named reviewer, no deployment approver), leaving the six that
  specify no decision authority named individually.
- standards/README.md: indexes already_a_crime_for_you.md and
  who_has_to_tell_you.md, both listed in the front-page contents but absent
  from the shelf's own index -- the latter carrying its hypothesis flag; and
  a standfirst pointing to research/ and the definition for what this shelf
  deliberately does not hold.

Tagged v3.4 text unchanged (E10).
